~ start
Darwin: These signs… they keep switching between true and false.
Logos: That is because you are now inside unstable logic. You must learn how truth behaves.

#Expanding Operators
Logos: You already know NOT, AND, and OR. Now you must understand how they behave in all situations.
Logos: A conjunction, P ∧ Q, is only true when both P and Q are true.
Logos: A disjunction, P ∨ Q, is false only when both P and Q are false.
Darwin: So I need to check both values every time.
Logos: Exactly.

#Intro to Conditional
Logos: You will now encounter conditionals.
Logos: A conditional, written as P → Q, means ‘if P happens, then Q must follow.’
Darwin: When does it fail?
Logos: It fails only when P is true and Q is false.

#Special Operators
Logos: A biconditional, P ↔ Q, is true only when both sides have the same truth value.
Logos: An exclusive OR, P ⊕ Q, is true only when exactly one is true.

#Classifying Statements
Logos: You must also classify statements.
Logos: A tautology is always true.
Logos: A contradiction is always false.
Logos: A contingency can be either true or false.
Darwin: So truth isn't random... it follows pattern.
Logos: And now you are beginning to see them.

#Evaluation
Logos: Answer these before you proceed.

~ question_1
Logos: First: When is P ∧ Q true?

- A. When one is true
	Logos: Incorrect. AND requires both to be true.
	=> question_1

- B. When both are true
	Logos: Correct.
	=> question_2

- C. When both are false
	Logos: Incorrect. AND requires both to be true.
	=> question_1

- D. When P is true only
	Logos: Incorrect. AND requires both to be true.
	=> question_1


~ question_2
Logos: Second: When is P ∨ Q false?

- A. When at least one is true
	Logos: Incorrect. OR fails only when both are false.
	=> question_2

- B. When both are false
	Logos: Correct.
	=> question_3

- C. When both are true
	Logos: Incorrect. OR is true if at least one is true.
	=> question_2

- D. When P is true only
	Logos: Incorrect. OR is still true if at least one is true.
	=> question_2


~ question_3
Logos: Third: When does P → Q fail?

- A. When P is false and Q is true
	Logos: Incorrect. That is valid implication.
	=> question_3

- B. When both are true
	Logos: Incorrect. That is valid implication.
	=> question_3

- C. When P is true and Q is false
	Logos: Correct.
	=> question_4

- D. When both are false
	Logos: Incorrect. That is valid implication.
	=> question_3


~ question_4
Logos: Fourth: What is a tautology?

- A. Always false
	Logos: Incorrect. That is contradiction.
	=> question_4

- B. Sometimes true
	Logos: Incorrect. That is contingency.
	=> question_4

- C. Always true
	Logos: Correct.
	Logos: You may now proceed.
	=> END

- D. Depends on variables
	Logos: Incorrect. That is contingency.
	=> question_4